      "id": "lpic1-tree-private-stage",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "unit": "linux-recursive-handovers",
      "topic": "Recursive directory handovers",
      "title": "Create a private working tree",
      "summary": "Copy an entire project tree with private defaults while preserving its source.",
      "objective": "Create /work/private-copy as a complete independent copy of /work/incoming, including nested, hidden and empty entries. Retain each source entry’s owner permission bits on the copy; remove group and other access. Transferred entries belong to alex, primary group staff. Set the simulated session’s creation mask so later ordinary files and folders also default to owner-only access. Preserve the source, its contents and permissions, and every unrelated supplied entry; leave no scratch copies.",
      "retry_guidance": "Inspect the failing outcome, repair the working files, then check again. Reset restores only this attempt if you need the supplied source back.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 18,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Investigate and repair",
        "Verify the complete handover"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Copy a directory, not just its visible files",
          "why": "A directory tree includes nested folders, empty folders and hidden names. cp -R copies the named directory and everything below it. Copying only the files shown by ordinary ls misses important parts.",
          "action": "Inspect the practice template before copying it.",
          "commands": [
            {
              "command": "find /practice/template -type f",
              "explanation": "Compare the surviving names.",
              "simulator_only": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Choose access before creating the copy",
          "why": "umask removes permission bits from newly created entries; it does not add permissions or repair existing copies. 077 removes group and other access. A source file with 644 becomes 600; a source directory with 755 becomes 700. A read-only source with 444 remains read-only at 400.",
          "action": "Set the practice creation mask, then copy the complete template to the unused working path.",
          "commands": [
            {
              "command": "umask 077",
              "explanation": "Set defaults for later new entries; existing files do not change.",
              "simulator_only": false
            },
            {
              "command": "cp -R /practice/template /practice/working",
              "explanation": "Copy the named practice files or complete directory.",
              "simulator_only": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Inspect content and access",
          "why": "The person running a normal new copy becomes its owner and uses their primary group; source ownership is not preserved. find checks nested names, ls -la shows hidden names and modes, and cat inspects exact file content. The source must remain unchanged.",
          "action": "Inspect the copy, including hidden names. Check the example, change its mask to 022 and observe that changing the mask does not alter existing entries.",
          "commands": [
            {
              "command": "ls -ld /practice/working",
              "explanation": "Inspect the copied access.",
              "simulator_only": false
            },
            {
              "command": "ls -l /practice/working",
              "explanation": "Inspect the copied access.",
              "simulator_only": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "A directory tree includes nested folders, empty folders and hidden names. cp -R copies the named directory and everything below it. Copying only the files shown by ordinary ls misses important parts. umask removes permission bits from newly created entries; it does not add permissions or repair existing copies. 077 removes group and other access. A source file with 644 becomes 600; a source directory with 755 becomes 700. A read-only source with 444 remains read-only at 400. The person running a normal new copy becomes its owner and uses their primary group; source ownership is not preserved. find checks nested names, ls -la shows hidden names and modes, and cat inspects exact file content. The source must remain unchanged.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare the complete source and destination with find and ls -la. Check nested and hidden entries as well as visible files.",
        "The creation mask affects new entries only. Inspect existing modes with ls -l; do not assume changing umask repairs a permissive copy.",
        "The worked approach is available on request. Verify exact contents and permissions, and preserve similarly named unrelated paths."
      ],
      "sources": [
